WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
There are lots of good reasons
to try and follow a healthier diet—

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.480
you lose weight,
you feel good,

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:09.280
but the main reason?

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:13.800
To live a longer, happier,
more productive life.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.840
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.760
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.720
Today, we look at bone
mineral density screening,

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.000
the safety and efficacy of
oral and injective medications,

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.600
which foods may protect
our bones, and more.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:33.920
Nearly one in five adults in the world may&nbsp;
have osteoporosis—that's hundreds of millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:40.400
of people. The word "osteoporosis"&nbsp;
literally means porous bone. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.120
most of our bone is actually porous to begin&nbsp;
with. Bone mineral density is considered to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:50.360
be the standard measure for the diagnosis of&nbsp;
osteoporosis, although the bone mineral density&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:55.800
cutoff for an osteoporosis diagnosis is kind&nbsp;
of arbitrary. Using the standard definition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:02.120
osteoporosis may affect about one in 10&nbsp;
women by age 60, two in 10 by age 74,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:10.760
and six or seven out of 10 by age 90. Osteoporosis&nbsp;
is typically thought of as a disease in women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:16.000
but one-third of hip fractures occur in&nbsp;
men. The lifetime risk for osteoporotic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:25.560
fractures for 50-year-old white women and men&nbsp;
is 40% and 13.3% respectively. The good news&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:31.120
is that osteoporosis need not occur. Based on a&nbsp;
study of the largest twin registry in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:37.280
less than 30% of osteoporotic fracture risk is&nbsp;
heritable, leading the researchers to conclude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:43.760
prevention of fractures, even in the oldest&nbsp;
elderly, should focus on lifestyle interventions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:48.600
This is consistent with the enormous variation&nbsp;
of hip fracture rates around the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:54.120
with the incidence of hip fracture varying&nbsp;
tenfold or even a hundredfold between countries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.120 --> 00:02:00.240
suggesting that excessive bone loss is not&nbsp;
just an inevitable consequence of aging.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.720
The United States Preventive Services Task&nbsp;
Force, an independent scientific panel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.760
that sets evidence-based clinical prevention&nbsp;
guidelines, recommends osteoporosis screening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:16.320
such as DEXA bone mineral density scan, for all&nbsp;
women aged 65 and older, and potentially starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.960
even earlier than 65 for women at increased&nbsp;
risk, such as those with a parental history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:27.920
of hip fracture, smokers, excessive alcohol&nbsp;
consumers, or those with low body weight.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:32.560
What should you do if you're diagnosed,&nbsp;
or more importantly, what should you do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:39.200
to never be diagnosed? Before exploring&nbsp;
the drugs offered to treat osteoporosis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:45.320
there are some drugs that may cause it. So&nbsp;
let's start there. Stomach acid-blocking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:50.840
proton pump inhibitor drugs, so-called&nbsp;
PPIs with brand names like Prilosec,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:56.280
Prevacid, Nexium, Protonix, and AcipHex, are&nbsp;
among the most popular drugs in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:03.040
raking in billions of dollars a year. But then&nbsp;
in 2006, two observational studies out of Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.560
suggested an association between intake of&nbsp;
this class of drugs and increased risk of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:13.080
hip fracture. By 2010, the growing evidence&nbsp;
forced the US Food and Drug Administration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:20.720
to issue a safety alert implicating PPI use with&nbsp;
fractures of the hip, wrist, and spine. By now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:25.640
there have been dozens of such studies involving&nbsp;
more than two million people that overall show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:32.760
higher hip fracture rates among both long-&nbsp;
and short-term users at all dose levels.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:37.840
The irony is that most people taking these&nbsp;
drugs shouldn't even be on them in the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:43.480
place. These PPIs are only FDA approved for&nbsp;
10 days of use for the treatment of H. pylori,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:49.320
up to two weeks for heartburn, up to 8 weeks for&nbsp;
acid reflux disease, and for two to six months for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:56.120
ulcers. Yet, in a community survey, most users&nbsp;
remained on these drugs for more than a year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.120 --> 00:04:01.880
and more than 60% of patients were taking them for&nbsp;
inappropriate reasons, often wrongly prescribed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:08.720
for "indigestion," for instance. Calls to stop&nbsp;
this massive overuse from regulatory authorities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.920
have fallen on deaf doctor ears, and now&nbsp;
that they're available over the counter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:19.840
the problem of overuse may have just gotten&nbsp;
even worse. They can be hard to stop taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:25.360
since many patients experience withdrawal&nbsp;
symptoms that can last for weeks. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:29.880
if you take normal healthy volunteers without&nbsp;
any symptoms and put them on these drugs for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:34.080
2 months and then covertly switch them&nbsp;
to a placebo without their knowledge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:39.800
all of a sudden they can develop acid-related&nbsp;
symptoms such as heartburn or acid regurgitation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:44.880
ending up worse off than they were before&nbsp;
they even started taking the drugs.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.800
In addition to bone fractures, this class&nbsp;
of drugs has also been linked to increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:54.440
risk of other possible long-term&nbsp;
adverse effects, such as pneumonia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.440 --> 00:05:00.720
intestinal infections, kidney failure, stomach&nbsp;
cancer, and cardiovascular disease. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.240
the blood vessel effects could explain&nbsp;
the case report of abrupt onset profound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:11.600
erectile dysfunction in a healthy young man&nbsp;
after initiating over-the-counter Prilosec&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:19.040
and also premature death. There are individuals&nbsp;
with conditions like Zollinger-Ellison syndrome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.560
stricken with tumors that can cause&nbsp;
excess stomach acid secretion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:28.040
for whom the risk versus benefit of long-term use&nbsp;
may be acceptable. But that's a far cry from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:34.000
hundred million PPIs prescribed annually in the&nbsp;
United States alone to deal with acid reflux.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:40.600
Without drugs, recommendations include weight&nbsp;
loss, smoking cessation, avoiding fatty meals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:45.160
especially within 2 to 3 hours of&nbsp;
bedtime, increased fiber consumption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:49.240
and an overall more plant-based&nbsp;
diet because non-vegetarianism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:55.000
is associated with twice the odds of&nbsp;
acid reflux-induced inflammation. Other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:59.360
classes of drugs associated with hip&nbsp;
fracture risk include antidepressants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:05.800
antiparkinson's drugs, antianxiety drugs,&nbsp;
oral corticosteroids, and the other major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:12.520
class of heartburn drugs, the H2 blockers&nbsp;
such as Pepcid, Zantac, Tagamet, and Axid.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:19.480
Excessive bone loss is not just an&nbsp;
inevitable consequence of aging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:23.960
and one of the things we can do to lower&nbsp;
our risk and that of our patients is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:31.560
take into account the potential skeletal side&nbsp;
effects of the drugs we take and prescribe.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:40.200
What about the drugs prescribed to treat&nbsp;
osteoporosis? First, let me address efficacy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:44.720
Drug therapy for osteoporosis is recommended&nbsp;
for those 50 and older with a history of past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:51.040
hip or spine fractures, those with hip or spine&nbsp;
T-scores of -2.5 or less, and postmenopausal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:56.040
women or men aged 50 and older who don't meet that&nbsp;
cutoff but have an estimated 20% or higher risk of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:01.040
major osteoporotic fracture over the subsequent&nbsp;
decade or an estimated 3% or greater risk of hip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:07.600
fracture. Specifically, a T-score is a measure of&nbsp;
how dense your bones are compared to a 30-year-old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:13.680
white woman, since that's the standard. We tend&nbsp;
to lose bone as we age. You can be labeled as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:19.520
having osteoporosis even if you have completely&nbsp;
normal bone density for your age. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:23.840
just because your bone density may be normal&nbsp;
doesn't mean it's necessarily optimal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:27.560
which is why the National Osteoporosis&nbsp;
Foundation set out these guidelines for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:33.520
drug treatment. Though another reason, perhaps,&nbsp;
is because it gets substantial funding from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:39.400
drug industry which rakes in literally billions&nbsp;
of dollars in profits from osteoporosis drugs.

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:46.880
What does the science say? The primary drug class&nbsp;
used to treat osteoporosis is the bisphosphonates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:53.480
sold under brand names such as Fosamax, Actonel,&nbsp;
Boniva, and Reclast. They're most effective at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:58.000
reducing vertebral fractures, cutting the&nbsp;
risk in postmenopausal women from 2.8% down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:05.920
to 1.44%. That's a relative risk reduction of&nbsp;
50% but an absolute risk reduction of only 1.4%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:10.000
meaning you'd have to treat 71 women to&nbsp;
prevent just one woman from getting a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:15.720
vertebral fracture. Unfortunately, the diagnosis&nbsp;
of vertebral fracture is kind of wishy-washy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:20.320
and depending on how you define the changes on&nbsp;
an x-ray, the prevalence of vertebral fractures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:29.520
can vary as much as 3% to 90% in the same elderly&nbsp;
population—almost none to almost all. They're all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:34.600
so poorly predictive of back pain or disability.&nbsp;
Vertebral fractures can certainly lead to back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:39.320
pain and reduced physical function, but only&nbsp;
about a third are symptomatic at all. The most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:45.720
harmful fractures are hip fractures. Despite most&nbsp;
of the clinical trials for osteoporosis treatment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:50.880
being funded by the drug companies themselves, no&nbsp;
primary prevention benefit from bisphosphonates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:56.600
has been found for hip fractures. In other words,&nbsp;
taking these drugs has not been convincingly shown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.600 --> 00:09:02.720
to prevent fracturing your hip in the first place.&nbsp;
However, having a prior fracture doubles your risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:06.960
of breaking another bone, so for those at high&nbsp;
risk, these bisphosphonate drugs may decrease&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:13.040
the odds of hip fracture by 25%. So, the absolute&nbsp;
risk reduction is again only about 1%. It may take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:18.920
treating 91 people for 3 years to prevent one&nbsp;
hip fracture. An overconfidence in the power of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:23.160
pills and procedures for disease prevention may&nbsp;
be one of the reasons doctors and patients alike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:28.840
may undervalue diet and lifestyle approaches. In a&nbsp;
study of those undergoing bone density scans, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:33.320
average yearly fracture risk that would motivate&nbsp;
most participants to consider preventive medicine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:39.920
was 50% to 60%, much higher than their actual&nbsp;
risk. Most patients want to be told the truth;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:44.040
they want to be told what the chances are that&nbsp;
the drugs will actually benefit them. But there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:49.200
a tension between the patient's right to know&nbsp;
and the likely reduction in their willingness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:55.640
to take the drug if they knew the truth. In&nbsp;
the bone density scan survey, participants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.640 --> 00:10:00.160
greatly overestimate their own personal risk for&nbsp;
hip fracture, thinking it was around 19% over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:05.920
the next 5 years, which is more than 10 times&nbsp;
higher than their actual risk of 1.4%. Rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:11.520
than disabusing them of this overestimate, some&nbsp;
suggest physicians should do the opposite—stoke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:17.440
fear in patients who refuse to comply with&nbsp;
medication recommendations by increasing their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:22.280
perceived susceptibility to fragility fractures&nbsp;
as well as their perceived severity of suffering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:27.840
from a fragility fracture. Since emotion can&nbsp;
be more motivating than reason and anecdotal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:33.720
evidence can be more effective than evidence, a&nbsp;
graphic scenario of suffering and incapacitation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:40.600
after hip fracture will enhance emotional&nbsp;
perception of this threat. But hip fractures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:45.440
can indeed be devastating and are associated&nbsp;
with a significant risk of ensuing death.

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:50.360
So, what about all the lives these drugs must be&nbsp;
saving? Well, only about a quarter of the deaths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:55.200
following fractures may be attributable to the&nbsp;
fracture itself, so most of the mortality risk may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:59.840
just be a consequence of the comorbidities—that&nbsp;
is, the other diseases and poor health status—of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:04.800
people who tend to fracture their hips. So, they&nbsp;
might have died anyway in that same timeframe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:12.240
even if they hadn't broken any bone. This may&nbsp;
help explain why osteoporosis drug treatments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:17.680
particularly bisphosphonates, fail to&nbsp;
significantly reduce overall mortality.

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:23.280
Okay, so the efficacy of the primary&nbsp;
drug class used to treat osteoporosis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:29.640
is so slight on an individual basis that&nbsp;
most patients, if truly fully informed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:37.360
likely would not take them. And that's&nbsp;
before even talking about the side effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:43.600
which I'll turn to right now. Potential lack of&nbsp;
efficacy is not the only reason why most people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:48.880
prescribed bisphosphonate drugs for osteoporosis&nbsp;
like Fosamax, Actonel, Boniva, and Reclast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:55.480
may stop taking them within a year. They're too&nbsp;
rare but devastating side effects—osteonecrosis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.480 --> 00:12:01.120
of the jaw and atypical femur fractures—that&nbsp;
contributed to around a 50% drop in the use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:05.240
of this class of drugs when they came to light.&nbsp;
The New York Times article noting the decline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:11.920
explained the reasoning: reports of the drugs&nbsp;
causing jawbones to rot and thigh bones to snap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:16.560
in two have shaken many osteoporosis patients&nbsp;
so much that they say they would rather take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:22.160
their chances with the disease. The jaw rot&nbsp;
syndrome that prevents many from initiating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:27.760
treatment for fear of their jaw falling off can&nbsp;
severely impact many aspects of quality of life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:34.080
but is exceedingly rare, affecting at most one&nbsp;
in a thousand patients treated for osteoporosis.

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:40.440
Atypical femur fractures can occur more frequently&nbsp;
though, as many as one in 300 users treated for 3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:45.280
years. They're called atypical because&nbsp;
they occur not after a fall or trauma,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:49.160
but just during routine activities&nbsp;
like walking, twisting at the waist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:56.440
or even just standing still. Your femur, your&nbsp;
thigh bone, the biggest bone in your body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.440 --> 00:13:04.240
just breaks in half—too cruel an irony from&nbsp;
a drug that's supposed to protect your bones.

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:08.600
These bisphosphonates work by inhibiting&nbsp;
the action of a type of bone cell called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:14.000
osteoclasts. Your entire skeleton is constantly&nbsp;
being remodeled, with bone added in some spots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:19.040
and taken away in others to conform to changing&nbsp;
demand. The cells that are continually laying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:22.920
down new bone are called osteoblasts,&nbsp;
and the ones that chisel away old bone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:28.680
are called osteoclasts. It makes sense then that&nbsp;
curbing the osteoclasts could prevent bone loss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:34.800
but by reducing the active remodeling process,&nbsp;
the bisphosphonates can freeze the skeleton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:40.920
allowing for the accumulation of microcracks&nbsp;
over time, resulting in stress fractures.

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:47.600
Other osteoporosis drugs, like denosumab, sold as&nbsp;
Prolia, prevent the same formation of osteoclasts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:53.640
in the first place and have been plagued by the&nbsp;
same kinds of rare but disturbing side effects. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:58.520
with anything in life, it all comes down to risks&nbsp;
versus benefits. I mean, how many hip fractures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.520 --> 00:14:03.920
are prevented for every femur that snaps? It&nbsp;
depends on your race and how long you're on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:09.720
these drugs. After 5 years, in white women, 36&nbsp;
hip fractures are prevented for every atypical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:16.080
femur fracture. Hispanic women only get half&nbsp;
the benefit, about 18 to 1, and for Asian women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:21.720
it's only about five hip fractures prevented for&nbsp;
every femur fracture caused. The study claims it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:28.440
failed to accrue enough data on black women. At&nbsp;
10 years of drug exposure, the ratios get worse—16&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:34.040
to 1 for white women, 5 to 1 for Hispanic&nbsp;
women, and only 1.5 to 1 for Asian women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:41.480
meaning that the devastating fractures prevented&nbsp;
and caused in Asian women are nearly comparable.

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:45.720
A nationwide survey of resident physicians&nbsp;
found that knowledge regarding osteoporosis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:50.200
diagnosis and treatment was poor, with a&nbsp;
particularly striking lack of knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:56.600
regarding the serious drug side effects. The&nbsp;
good news is that after stopping the drugs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.600 --> 00:15:02.720
the risk of femur fracture rapidly dropped by&nbsp;
70% within a year, leading to the suggestion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:09.320
that a drug holiday be considered after a few&nbsp;
years on the drug to help mediate the risk.

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:18.280
How crazy is that atypical femur fracture data,&nbsp;
right? I just cannot get over the irony of it all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.280 --> 00:15:24.640
Okay, so between the lack of sufficient efficacy&nbsp;
and these rare but devastating side effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:30.920
what can we do personally and how can we counsel&nbsp;
our patients to protect their skeletons? In 12&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:35.440
short years, government panels have gone from&nbsp;
suggesting widespread calcium supplementation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:44.360
may be necessary to protect our bones&nbsp;
to "do not supplement." What happened?

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:49.440
It all started with a 2008 study in New&nbsp;
Zealand. Short-term studies had shown that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:55.600
calcium supplementation may drop blood pressure&nbsp;
by about a point. The effect appears transient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.600 --> 00:16:00.920
disappearing after a few months—better than&nbsp;
nothing. And excess calcium in the gut can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:07.360
cause fat malabsorption by forming soap&nbsp;
fat, reducing saturated fat absorption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:12.960
and increasing fecal saturated fat content.&nbsp;
And indeed, if you take a couple Tums along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:17.080
with your half bucket of KFC, up to twice&nbsp;
as much fat could end up in your stool.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:21.640
And with less saturated fat absorbed into&nbsp;
your system, your cholesterol might drop.

00:16:21.640 --> 00:16:27.360
So, the New Zealand researchers were expecting&nbsp;
to lower heart attack rates by giving women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:34.480
calcium supplements. To their surprise, there&nbsp;
appeared to be more heart attacks in the calcium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:41.480
supplement group. Was this just a fluke? All&nbsp;
eyes turned to the Women's Health Initiative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.480 --> 00:16:47.080
the largest and longest randomized control&nbsp;
trial of calcium supplementation. The name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.080 --> 00:16:50.600
might sound familiar—that's the study&nbsp;
that uncovered how dangerous hormone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.600 --> 00:16:55.520
replacement therapy was. Would it&nbsp;
do the same for calcium supplements?

00:16:55.520 --> 00:17:01.040
The Women's Health Initiative&nbsp;
reported no adverse effects. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:04.440
the majority of participants were&nbsp;
already taking calcium supplements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:09.560
before the study started. So effectively, the&nbsp;
study was just comparing higher versus lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:14.560
dose calcium supplementation, not calcium&nbsp;
supplements versus no calcium supplements.

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:19.360
What if you go back and just see what happened to&nbsp;
the women who started out not taking supplements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.360 --> 00:17:24.840
and then were randomized to the supplement&nbsp;
group? Those who started calcium supplements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.840 --> 00:17:31.400
suffered significantly more heart attacks&nbsp;
or strokes. Thus, high dose or low dose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.400 --> 00:17:36.920
any calcium supplementation seems to&nbsp;
increase cardiovascular disease risk.

00:17:36.920 --> 00:17:41.640
So, researchers went back digging through&nbsp;
other trial data for heart attack and stroke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:45.240
rates in women randomized to calcium&nbsp;
supplements with or without vitamin D&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:51.680
added and confirmed the danger. And most&nbsp;
of the population studies agreed—users of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.680 --> 00:17:57.560
calcium supplements tended to have increased&nbsp;
rates of heart disease, stroke, and death.

00:17:57.560 --> 00:18:03.760
The supplement industry was not happy,&nbsp;
accusing the researchers of relying in part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:07.760
on self-reported data—like they just asked&nbsp;
people if they had a heart attack or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.760 --> 00:18:14.080
rather than verifying it. And indeed, long-term&nbsp;
calcium supplementation causes all sorts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.080 --> 00:18:19.480
gastrointestinal distress, including twice the&nbsp;
risk of being hospitalized with acute symptoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:26.200
that may have been confused with a heart&nbsp;
attack. But no, the increased risk was seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:31.400
consistently across the trials, whether&nbsp;
the heart attacks were verified or not.

00:18:31.400 --> 00:18:36.840
Okay, but why do calcium supplements increase&nbsp;
heart attack risk but not calcium you get in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:41.920
your diet? Perhaps because when you take&nbsp;
calcium pills, you get a spike of calcium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:46.560
in your bloodstream that you don't get just&nbsp;
eating calcium-rich foods. Within hours of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:52.560
taking supplemental calcium, the calcium levels&nbsp;
in the blood shoot up and can stay up as long as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:59.080
8 hours. This evidently produces what's called&nbsp;
a hypercoagulable state—your blood clots more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:04.800
easily, which could increase the risk of&nbsp;
clots in the heart and brain. And indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:09.000
higher calcium blood levels are tied to&nbsp;
higher heart attack and stroke rates.

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:14.160
So, the mechanism may be calcium supplements&nbsp;
leading to unnaturally large, rapid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.160 --> 00:19:19.640
and sustained calcium levels in the blood, which&nbsp;
can have a variety of potentially problematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.640 --> 00:19:26.280
effects. Calcium supplements have been widely&nbsp;
embraced on the grounds that they are natural and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.280 --> 00:19:31.720
therefore a safe way of preventing osteoporosis&nbsp;
fractures. But it's now becoming clear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.720 --> 00:19:37.320
taking calcium in one or two daily doses is not&nbsp;
natural and that it does not reproduce the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:43.400
metabolic effects as calcium in food the way&nbsp;
nature intended. And furthermore, the evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:49.800
is also becoming steadily stronger that calcium&nbsp;
supplementation may not be safe. That's why most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:54.240
organizations providing advice regarding&nbsp;
bone health now recommend that individuals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.240 --> 00:20:00.120
should obtain their calcium requirement&nbsp;
from diet in preference to supplements.

